### What is layered architecture in Spring Boot?

Layered architecture means splitting your application into distinct layers where each layer has one job. In Spring Boot the standard layers are Controller, Service, and Repository. The Controller handles HTTP concerns: parsing request bodies, extracting path variables, and returning the correct status code. The Service handles business logic: calculating interest, applying transfer limits, and orchestrating multiple operations. The Repository handles data access: reading and writing to the database.

The key rule is that each layer talks only to the layer directly below it. The Controller calls the Service. The Service calls the Repository. The Controller never talks to the Repository directly.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.service.AccountService;
import com.bank.model.AccountStatement;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/accounts")
public class AccountController {

    private final AccountService accountService;

    public AccountController(AccountService accountService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/{accountId}/balance")
    public ResponseEntity<BigDecimal> getBalance(@PathVariable String accountId) {
        // Controller does NOT query the database directly.
        // It delegates to the Service layer.
        BigDecimal balance = accountService.getBalance(accountId);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(balance);
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (HTTP response):
HTTP 200 OK
Content-Type: application/json

250000.00
```

### Why is putting database logic in a controller bad practice?

When Rajesh puts a JDBC call or JPA query directly inside a controller method, several problems appear. First, testing becomes painful. You cannot test the controller without a running database because the database code is embedded in the HTTP handling code. With proper layering, Rajesh tests the controller using MockMvc and a mocked service, with no database needed at all.

Second, code reuse disappears. If another controller or a scheduled job needs the same query, the developer must copy the database code. With a Repository, both the controller and the scheduled job call the same Repository method.

Third, transaction management breaks. Spring's `@Transactional` annotation belongs on Service methods where business operations are coordinated. A controller should not manage transactions because a single business operation might involve multiple repository calls that must either all succeed or all roll back together.

```java
package com.bank.controller;

// WRONG: database logic inside the controller
@RestController
public class BadAccountController {

    @Autowired
    private EntityManager entityManager;

    @GetMapping("/api/v1/accounts/{id}/balance")
    public BigDecimal getBalance(@PathVariable String id) {
        // This is untestable without a database.
        // Transaction management is missing.
        // This query cannot be reused by other classes.
        return (BigDecimal) entityManager
            .createQuery("SELECT a.balance FROM Account a WHERE a.id = :id")
            .setParameter("id", id)
            .getSingleResult();
    }
}
```

```java
package com.bank.controller;

import com.bank.service.AccountService;

// CORRECT: controller delegates to service
@RestController
public class GoodAccountController {

    private final AccountService accountService;

    public GoodAccountController(AccountService accountService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
    }

    @GetMapping("/api/v1/accounts/{id}/balance")
    public ResponseEntity<BigDecimal> getBalance(@PathVariable String id) {
        BigDecimal balance = accountService.getBalance(id);
        return ResponseEntity.ok(balance);
    }
}
```

### What are the benefits of the DAO (Repository) pattern?

The Data Access Object pattern, called Repository in Spring, abstracts persistence behind an interface. The Service layer depends on the interface, not on the implementation. This gives three concrete benefits.

First, you can swap the database engine without changing business logic. If National Bank of India migrates from Oracle to PostgreSQL, only the Repository implementation changes. The Service and Controller are untouched.

Second, each layer is independently testable. The Controller is tested with MockMvc and a mocked Service with no database running. The Service is tested with a mocked Repository and no database running. The Repository is tested with `@DataJpaTest` and a real test database.

Third, the DAO centralizes all SQL and query logic in one place, making it easy to optimize queries, add indexes, or switch from JPA to JDBC for a specific query without scattering changes across the codebase.

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Optional;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    Optional<Account> findByAccountNumber(String accountNumber);

    @Query("SELECT a.balance FROM Account a WHERE a.accountNumber = :accountNumber")
    BigDecimal findBalanceByAccountNumber(String accountNumber);
}
```

### How does Spring Boot auto-configuration work internally?

When you annotate your main class with `@SpringBootApplication`, it includes `@EnableAutoConfiguration`. At startup, Spring Boot reads the `META-INF/spring/org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports` files from every JAR on the classpath. Each listed class is a `@Configuration` class guarded by `@Conditional` annotations.

For example, when `spring-boot-starter-data-jpa` is on the classpath and `spring.datasource.url` is set in properties, the auto-configuration creates a HikariCP DataSource, an EntityManagerFactory, a TransactionManager, and a Flyway migration runner, all without any explicit `@Bean` definitions from the developer.

The `@Conditional` annotations are the gatekeepers:

- `@ConditionalOnClass(DataSource.class)` activates when the DataSource class exists on the classpath.
- `@ConditionalOnProperty(name = "spring.datasource.url")` activates when the property is set.
- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean(DataSource.class)` backs off if you define your own DataSource bean.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import com.zaxxer.hikari.HikariDataSource;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.condition.ConditionalOnMissingBean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Bean;
import org.springframework.context.annotation.Configuration;
import javax.sql.DataSource;

@Configuration
public class CustomDataSourceConfig {

    // This @Bean overrides auto-configured DataSource
    // because @ConditionalOnMissingBean on the auto-config sees this bean and backs off.
    @Bean
    public DataSource dataSource() {
        HikariDataSource ds = new HikariDataSource();
        ds.setJdbcUrl("jdbc:postgresql://prod-db:5432/nbi_accounts");
        ds.setUsername("nbi_app");
        ds.setMaximumPoolSize(20);
        ds.setConnectionTimeout(5000);
        return ds;
    }
}
```

### How do you debug auto-configuration?

When auto-configuration does not behave as expected, Rajesh has two tools. The first is the `--debug` flag. Running the application with `java -jar app.jar --debug` prints a Conditions Evaluation Report at startup showing every auto-configuration class and exactly why it matched (positive match) or was skipped (negative match).

The second tool is the Actuator endpoint `/actuator/conditions`, which provides the same information as a JSON response at runtime.

```bash
# Run with debug flag to see the Conditions Evaluation Report
java -jar national-bank-app.jar --debug
```

