### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer understands that auto-configuration order matters. Spring Boot processes auto-configuration classes in a defined order, and `@AutoConfigureAfter` and `@AutoConfigureBefore` control sequencing. If your custom DataSource bean must be available before Flyway auto-configuration runs, you use these annotations.

A senior also knows that `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` has a timing dependency: the condition is evaluated when the auto-configuration class is processed, so if your custom bean is registered later, the auto-configuration may not see it. Using `@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)` in auto-configuration classes avoids CGLIB proxying overhead for configuration classes that do not need inter-bean references.

In production at National Bank of India, Pankaj designed the microservices so that each service has its own custom auto-configuration module. Shared concerns like audit logging, correlation ID propagation, and health checks are packaged into an internal starter JAR (`nbi-spring-boot-starter-audit`) that downstream services include as a dependency. The starter's auto-configuration activates automatically via `@ConditionalOnClass` checks.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can a Controller call another Controller directly? No, controllers should not call each other. Extract shared logic into a Service.
- What happens if two `@Bean` methods create the same type? Spring throws a `BeanDefinitionOverrideException` unless you set `spring.main.allow-bean-definition-overriding=true`.
- Why use constructor injection over field injection? Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit, supports immutability with final fields, and enables testing without Spring.
- How does `@Transactional` interact with layering? It belongs on Service methods. Placing it on a Controller method works technically but violates separation of concerns because the controller now manages transaction boundaries.
